Mark Odell[050617]modellus@pathlink.com[050617][050617]> And there's a moral in there, somewhere.<BR><BR>IMCO the moral is, "Never assume what a law says; read what the statutes actually say for yourself, because the law is presumed to be knowable; always be skeptical of the government's proffered interpretation(s) of what a set of statutes means, because they'll always give you the interpretation that profits <B>them</B>; if the task of reading what the statutes actually say seems too onerous because "you need a lawyer to untangle the mess", then ask yourself if that "tangled mess" (bearing in mind the sheer quantity of rat-shrewdness as distinct from principled intelligence that went into its creation) is more likely to be accidental or deliberate, and if the latter, what possible motives its writers might have had for creating something deliberately obfuscated instead of clear and unambiguous; always be looking for the government's weak link."<BR><BR>> But it does sound like something to pass around!<BR><BR>I've <A HREF="http://www.lewrockwell.com/orig6/speiser2.html">done</A> <A HREF="http://www.lewrockwell.com/orig4/grigg3.html">my</A> <A HREF="http://www.lewrockwell.com/orig4/wiggins5.html">part</A>.<BR><BR>The key questions which IMCO everyone concerned about this matter should stay focused on are:<BR>* Why does "the step and the oath" still remain as part of the draft law?<BR>* Why does the available evidence indicate that the government has to have it in order for a given inductee's conscription to survive judicial review?<BR><BR>"The step and the oath" is the piece of the "draft puzzle" that just doesn't fit. And, ISTM that all but a few of the possible answers proffered to the question Why are falsifiable with little effort, especially those which studiously avoid any mention of or allusion to the 13th Amendment.<BR>[050617]06-18-2005 16:59
